\name{Ggplot2DirectedGraphLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2DirectedGraphLayerProcessor}
\title{Directed Graph Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads the nodes of a \code{\link[ggraph:ggraph]{ggraph::ggraph()}} drawing of a directed graph as the
\code{directed_graph} trace: one node per drawn point, each naming the nodes
whose edges arrive at it, so a reader walks the graph by its edges -- what
feeds a node, what it feeds, where the graph branches and merges.

A ggraph chart is an edge layer (\code{geom_edge_link()} and its siblings,
ggraph's own geoms), a node layer (\code{geom_node_point()}, a \code{GeomPoint}
over the node layout) and labels. The edge geoms matched no branch of the
adapter, and an unread layer drops the whole chart to a static image; read
as points, the nodes would say only where the layout put them. So for a
directed graph the edge layers are skipped and the node layer carries the
graph, read from the \code{igraph} object ggraph keeps on the layout.

Each node is highlighted as its own point: the node layer draws one point
per node, in node order, as one group of \verb{<use>} elements.

Emitted with \code{type = "directed_graph"}, which the core has read since
maidr 4.14.0.
}
\keyword{internal}
